 ],
 "literal": "In the hollow, sturdy, of the desert road, of the Ya tree / peeled trunk's whole base, having gored to pierce / with a strong large trunk, having pulled down, languishing gait / small-eyed large herd's intense hunger, which resolves / curved-tusked elephant, they saw, O friend! / Their duty to fulfill, having resolved, in every place / bound by the desire for wealth, who went / our desirable lover, the path he went!",
 "translation": "O friend! Along the path where our beloved went, bound by the desire for wealth to fulfill his duty, he would have seen the curved-tusked elephant. With its strong, large trunk, it pulls down and gores the sturdy, hollow-trunked Ya tree of the desert road, piercing its peeled base to feed and satisfy the intense hunger of its languishing, small-eyed herd.",
 "modern": "தோழியே! தன் கடமையை நிறைவேற்ற எண்ணி, விரும்பத்தக்க பொருளைத் தேடும் பிணிப்பினால் சென்ற நம்முடைய அன்பான காதலர் சென்ற வழியிலே, வளைந்த தந்தங்களை உடைய ஆண் யானையைக் கண்டிருப்பார். அது, பாலைவன வழியில் உள்ள பொந்துகளைக் கொண்ட, வயிரம்பாய்ந்த யா மரத்தின் பொரிந்த பட்டையையுடைய அடிமரத்தை தன் வலிமையான துதிக்கையினால் வளைத்து, தன் தந்தத்தால் குத்திப் பிளந்து, தளர்ந்த நடையையும் சிறிய கண்களையும் உடைய தன் பெரிய சுற்றத்தின் மிக்க பசியைத் தீர்க்கும்.",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the pālai (desert) landscape, where the theme is separation. The friend (tōḻi) speaks to the heroine (talaivi) to comfort her while the hero is away seeking wealth. She reassures the heroine by describing a scene the hero must be witnessing on his journey. In the arid desert, a male elephant uses its immense strength to tear down the bark of a sturdy Ya tree to feed its hungry, exhausted mate and herd. Seeing this display of love and responsibility, the hero will surely be reminded of his own duty to cherish and protect the heroine, prompting him to return quickly. The key words are yāṉai (elephant), paci tīrkkum (satisfying hunger), and kātalar ceṉṟa vāṟē (the path our lover went).",
